  "theory": {
    "intro": "Reading the inverse trig derivatives backwards gives two standard integrals. The shape $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}}$ integrates to $\\arcsin\\dfrac{x}{a}$, and $\\dfrac{1}{a^{2}+x^{2}}$ integrates to $\\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\dfrac{x}{a}$. Only the second carries a factor $\\dfrac{1}{a}$.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{dx}{\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}}=\\arcsin\\dfrac{x}{a}+C$, with no extra factor in front.",
      "$\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{dx}{a^{2}+x^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\dfrac{x}{a}+C$, where the $\\dfrac{1}{a}$ is essential.",
      "$\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{-dx}{\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}}=\\arccos\\dfrac{x}{a}+C$: the same shape with the sign reversed.",
      "Read $a$ as the square root of the constant, so $9+x^{2}$ has $a=3$.",
      "$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}}$ exists only for $|x|<a$, so the limits of a definite integral must lie strictly inside that interval.",
      "Take any constant factor outside the integral before matching the shape.",
      "When $x$ carries a coefficient, factor it out first, as in $\\sqrt{16-9x^{2}}=3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16}{9}-x^{2}}$.",
      "Reversing the chain rule gives $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f'(x)}{1+[f(x)]^{2}}\\,dx=\\arctan f(x)+C$.",
      "Every indefinite integral needs $+C$, and a definite integral does not.",
      "Check any primitive by differentiating it back."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Match the integrand to $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{a^{2}+x^{2}}$, and read off $a$.",
      "Take any constant factor outside the integral.",
      "Write the primitive: $\\arcsin\\dfrac{x}{a}+C$, or $\\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\dfrac{x}{a}+C$.",
      "For a definite integral, substitute the upper limit and subtract the value at the lower limit."
    ]
  },
